= Network interface controller — Technical =
'''Reading depth:''' [[Network interface controller/Simple|Simple]] · [[Network interface controller/Technical|Technical]]
<onlyinclude>A '''network interface controller''' (NIC) implements a host-to-network data path whose performance and correctness depend on MAC/PHY behavior, DMA descriptors and buffers, queue scheduling, interrupt or polling policy, host-memory bandwidth and operating-system processing. Link rate alone predicts neither application goodput nor tail latency.</onlyinclude>

== Standards and boundaries ==
IEEE 802.3-2022 defines Ethernet MAC/PHY behavior, not a universal NIC host-side DMA or PCIe queue architecture.<ref name="ieee">IEEE, ''IEEE Std 802.3-2022: IEEE Standard for Ethernet'', doi:10.1109/IEEESTD.2022.9844436, [https://standards.ieee.org/ieee/802.3/10422/ publisher record].</ref> The PHY can be separate or integrated. A NIC is hardware (sometimes with firmware), whereas the driver and network stack are software; a network also requires links and peers.

== Data path and DMA ownership ==
A representative transmit path is application → kernel network stack → driver Tx queue → device DMA read of host buffer → MAC/PHY → link; receive uses NIC DMA writes to host buffers, followed by host processing. Virtualization and kernel-bypass paths differ. Drivers allocate descriptor and data buffers, map DMA addresses, publish producer indices/doorbells, and reclaim completed buffers; Rx rings require replenishment. DMA does not remove CPU processing or memory traffic. Descriptor ownership, memory barriers, IOMMU translation and streaming versus coherent mappings are platform/device dependent; CPU pointers cannot substitute for DMA mappings.<ref name="dma">Linux kernel, [https://docs.kernel.org/core-api/dma-api-howto.html DMA API HOWTO].</ref>

== NAPI, queues and latency ==
Linux NAPI usually begins after interrupt notification and processes events via polling, though busy polling and threaded modes exist. A NAPI instance need not map one-to-one to a queue or interrupt. In the documented poll contract, the ''budget'' limits Rx packet work; Tx completion cleanup can proceed without consuming that Rx budget. Drivers must handle the budget-exhausted versus completion transition correctly.<ref name="napi">Linux kernel, [https://docs.kernel.org/networking/napi.html NAPI driver API and scheduling].</ref> Multiqueue devices can distribute flows and completions across CPUs; receive-side scaling and affinity affect cache locality. Interrupt moderation trades event overhead against waiting time; busy polling spends CPU cycles for potentially lower wakeup latency. Tail latency requires measurement rather than inference from average packet rate.

== Throughput bounds and assumptions ==
At nominal R=10×10^9 bits/s, R/8=1.25×10^9 bytes/s = '''1.25 GB/s''' decimal, an ideal line-rate byte conversion, not TCP/application goodput. If each transmitted packet consumes B=1000 bytes ''on wire'' (including the overhead chosen in that definition), the illustrative ceiling is R/(8B)=1.25×10^6 packets/s. It is not a benchmark. Ethernet preamble, interframe gap, frame check sequence, VLAN tags, IP/TCP headers, retransmissions and application framing must be accounted for consistently; do not use application payload bytes as B without adding the missing wire costs. For mean payload P and mean wire cost B, a simple bound is G_payload ≤ R(P/B), with other bottlenecks potentially lowering G further.

== Offloads, isolation and tradeoffs ==
Checksum and segmentation offloads, receive aggregation, RSS/steering and virtualization facilities can reduce selected host costs but are not universally supported. Packet captures may observe data before or after offload transformations. Firmware, driver revisions, IOMMU policies and DMA isolation affect security and reproducibility. Neither an IEEE standard nor a kernel API page benchmarks a vendor's NIC.

== Experimental methodology and open questions ==
Record NIC/PHY model, firmware and driver versions, link negotiation, MTU, frame-size distribution, offered load, queues, CPU/IRQ affinity, interrupt moderation, offloads, IOMMU and NUMA topology. Measure payload goodput, on-wire counters, packet loss, latency percentiles, cycles/packet, energy and fairness under sustained and burst traffic. Compare against explicit line-rate bounds and vary packet size and concurrency. Research questions include adaptive interrupt moderation under latency SLAs, queue fairness, secure DMA isolation, energy-per-bit optimization and programmable data-path verification; these are design questions, not established universal performance improvements.

